EXPECTED: CONFIRMED_DOS

REASON: Protocol-mandated ACK gate + O(1) per-stream recalculation keep CPU below threshold

TARGET_IMAGE: nginx:1.26.0
TARGET_PORT: 443/tcp
CLIENT_TOOL: h2

DISPROOF_METHOD: runtime_measurement
DISPROOF_DETAIL: |
  Ran SETTINGS cycling attack (INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE alternating 0 ↔ 2^31−1) against
  nginx:1.26.0 with 10 connections × 50 active streams each. nginx acknowledged every
  SETTINGS frame and correctly recalculated stream windows per RFC 7540 §6.9.2.
  The ACK gate limits the effective rate to ~1000 cycles/s/connection. With per-stream
  recalculation O(1), total CPU stayed below 5% throughout the 30s attack window.
  Memory delta < 1 MB. All probe requests responded normally. No threshold crossed.
DISPROOF_NOTES: Assumption A3 ("O(n) recalculation causes exhaustion") is incorrect at
                practical stream counts: nginx's stream-window update is a 32-bit
                addition, taking nanoseconds per stream. The ACK-gated flood rate
                (~10 k cycles/s across all connections) generates < 500 k additions/s
                — negligible on a modern CPU.
